      ]
      chapter: "Lens"
    
    
      notes: ["I'll introduce Data-Oriented Debugging by way of this example. This is an arithmetic calculator", "This calculator has a problem: when you put in 2 + 3 * 4^2, you get 26. For reference, if you're following order of operations, you should get 50.", "So you might think to yourself, \"I wonder if the order of operations is incorrect\".", "You can get at that by adding a print statement to the expression evaluator to print the operator as its executed. You know that they'll print out in order, which will let you infer the order that the operations were executed", "And when you do this, you can see that the operations do actually print out in the order that you expect, which is exponentiation, then multiplication, then addition", "Which allows you to conclude that the problem is not with the order of operations", "In Data-Oriented Debuggging, when you suspect that the order of operations is wrong but aren't able to tell whether this suspicion is true from looking at the code, we say you have a \"Data Need\" -- something that is answered by looking at some feature of a concrete execution of the code.", "You fulfill this data need by issuing a query using your debugging tool. Here, that's adding a print line", "You interpret the output that your debugging tool gives in response to your query, which is the terminal output here", "And by interpreting this output, you update your mental model of the program"]
    }
    
    
    Slide {
      label: "Place & Time — Calculator"
      notes: [
        "An on to the other half of the paper title, across Place and Time. If we think of debugging as involving querying a program's execution and getting some output, then it's helpful to have a way to talk about the structure of that execution data\n\nWe visualize this using the orthogonal axes of Place and Time\n\nPlace refers to all of the locations in the code and the variables contained there. You can kind of imagine the source code exploded vertically across this axis.\n\n"
      ]
